00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:05.510 align:start position:0%
 
sir how are you doing  good it's good to see you again have you  been  uh everything is going great when i get  to talk to you oh that's  very kind i mean that so sincerely we've  had some good interviews in the past i  thought  yeah listen it's hitting me right now  that you realize who i am  and that to me is [ __ ] crazy so  i'm just throwing that out there as a  huge fan of your work um so let me jump  in and say pig is fantastic  and you are so great in this role i'm  curious  um what was it about this project that  said i i want to do this this is  important to me  it was a screenplay  that i thought was written  with great sincerity uh  it had no other motivation  than to just tell a quiet story  of loss there were things about it that  reminded me of  a like almost like a japanese haiku  in meaning that you know five seven five  syllables that  what was said between the syllables  was as important or more important than  the words themselves  that it was a very evocative screenplay  and michael was a director who wanted to  tell a different kind of  story it was a meditation of sorts on  loss  and i was at a point in my own life  where it was kind of like no acting  please  i um i have  close relationships with my animals  brothers and sisters my cats my dogs  and um you know i would sometimes get  bad dreams  about losing my cat you know we've all  become very close to  our our animals after what we've been  through with the pandemic and quarantine  and um so i i didn't feel like it would  be  a huge challenge to to play this role  that it would come out  just very easily because i felt i had  the  relationships and the life experience to  tell the story  so that's what drew me to it i love  learning about the behind the scenes  of the making of a movie is there  anything that you think might surprise  people to learn about the making of pig  i don't know about surprise uh per se  i but i do think that a a tremendous  amount of  thought went into  conveying the joy of  of making a a  a dish a culinary an epicurean  experience  that can communicate  uh emotion and i had two excellent chefs  to work with uh chef chris of chef  ruckner from le pigeon and  they spent some time with me i'm very  passionate about food it's what i'm  interested in  food for me always came first then  music then uh film performance but  food to me is it's uh it's something i  have great respect for  and the regard for food all spiritual  level  of uh the the the power  of food um and i think that came through  especially in the filmmaking process  that michael had great regard  he had me read marco pierre white uh  devil in the kitchen and that was very  effective so a lot of thought went into  the food itself and how it would be  presented  i have to tell you that um one of my  most anticipated films  in the upcoming future is something with  you the unbearable weight of massive  talent  i could not be more excited for that  film if i could see it tomorrow i would  pay money  um what can you say about that project  because it sounds  awesome well i think what i can say  about the project  is that everybody involved uh tom gorman  kevin eden uh pedro pascal  everybody came into it with genuine  passion and care  uh that the movie was was nobody  uh pulled their punches or  or tried to uh sweep anything under the  rug  everything was approached with  tremendous  care um having said that  i i will probably never see the movie  myself  because the idea of going meta and  playing  a quote-unquote surrealistic version of  myself  and a quote-unquote surrealistic version  or interpretation of  a younger version of myself going  head-to-head it might just be  too psychologically uh  invasive for one nicolas cage  i i i but i i can tell you that it  came from the right place from all of us  and it and it has a lot of heart  and and the movie means well i know that  much  i heard the script was awesome it's a  great script and  and and it created quite a uh quite a  bit of uh  bidding business war that part i don't  get involved in but  but it did it did it was very evocative  for whatever reason that i can't explain  uh  it's inexplicable to me but nonetheless  it is  what it is and it happened so i  i will have to hear from you what you  think of it um  and uh i will go to the premiere but i'm  probably not going to be sitting in the  audience  i completely get it i mean this  sincerely pig is fantastic  and i really hope people um come out to  see it because you're working it  is so good thank you very much and happy  to speak with you again  um listen have a fantastic day thank you  so much for your time  thank you

